  "reason": "Complete construction and prerequisite audit; see research/phase-2-next-20-batch-4.notes.md and coverage.json. Source D, Theorem 4.2.8 p.220, explicit integrability-boundary witness; documented alternative-proof disposition, no raw fetch claim. Take Omega={(j,e): j>=1, e in {-1,1}} with mass 2^{-j-1} at each point. The nonnegative weighted-Dirac theorem gives a measure; its total mass is sum_{j>=1}2^{-j}=1, by finite geometric sums and their limit. Let F_0=sigma(j) be unions of the two-point j-blocks and F_n=power set for n>=1. Set M_0=0 and M_n=e for n>=1. On each block the two equally weighted signs have zero mean; summing absolutely over unions proves E[e|F_0]=0 by the event definition. Thereafter M is already known, so it is a bounded martingale. Define H_1=2^j and H_k=0 for k>=2; H is finite and predictable. The first algebraic gain is 2^j e, whose absolute expectation equals sum_{j>=1}1=infinity: finite truncations at j<=m have absolute integral m and MCT passes to the limit. Thus it fails precisely the product-integrability domain. Never write a signed conditional expectation of this nonintegrable gain. AC is inherited from the Radon–Nikodym construction of conditional expectation; if a sequence of versions is needed, AC selects measurable versions at the countably many times. Identities remain identities of almost-sure classes, without completing the filtration.",
